WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.660 --> 00:00:04.460
On January 3rd 1999

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:08.860
a man named Andrew Goldstein wandered onto
the New York City subway platform

00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:14.200
and shoved a stranger named Kendra Webdale
into the path of an oncoming train.

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:17.580
With the story making national news

00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:23.230
reporters dug into Golstein's past and found
that he had been diagnosed with schizophrenia

00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:25.960
and had a history of violent episodes.

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:28.660
He had been in and out of psychiatric facilities

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:32.500
but his caretakers repeatedly released him
back into the streets

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:37.000
against their better judgement because of
a shortage of available beds.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:40.300
The murder of Kendra Webdale brought attention
to our inability

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:44.640
to provide adequate treatment to Americans
with severe mental health problems.

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.700
Which 20 years later is still ongoing.

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:52.500
Prisons and jails are filled with inmates
exhibiting symptoms of mental illness

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:53.880
and in need of treatment.

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:56.720
Many of the homeless people crowding the streets
of cities

00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:59.499
like Los Angeles, San Francisco and New York

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:02.040
are mentally ill and in need of care.

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:04.960
And violent episodes like the murder of Kendra
Webdale

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:10.760
have brought renewed calls to bring back one
of America's most controversial institutions.

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:12.500
The mental hospital.

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:18.980
And to entrust the state with more authority
to force psychiatric care on patients against their will.

00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:24.900
Some psychiatrists and bioethicists have proposed
'bring back the psychiatric hospital.'

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:27.180
You know in the old days we had mental institutions

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:32.720
and you could nab somebody like this because,
you did, they knew he was, something was off.

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:37.440
The parents of mentally ill patients are among
the most vocal advocates.

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:39.900
They shut down all the hospitals

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:45.120
because oh we have all these medicines now
and everybody can be in recovery.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:49.040
Well the unfortunate fact is not everybody
recovers.

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:51.320
So where do they go?

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:53.080
What do they do?

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:55.500
So is it time to bring back the asylum?

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:58.920
And why did it disappear in the first place?

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:03.680
My contention is that there is no mental illness
and there can be no mental illness.

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:07.480
In the mid 20th century the libertarian psychiatrist
Thomas Szasz

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:12.660
first articulated a theory that would rock
the profession and define his career.

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:16.380
That the whole concept of mental illness was
a fraud.

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:22.680
Thomas Szasz was a psychiatrist who didn't
really believe in psychiatry.

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:25.680
Having a book by him on your shelf is enough
to start an argument.

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:30.060
Jacob Sullum interviewed Szasz for Reason
magazine in 2000

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:33.040
and cites him as a major influence on his
own work.

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:38.960
He argued what people call mental illness
is not a literal disease

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:40.520
but a metaphorical disease

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:43.880
What he called problems in living, internal
conflicts, conflicts with yourself,

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:44.960
conflicts with other people.

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:47.900
You could ask people to define mental illness
and no one could.

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:51.300
One could only give examples of it, like homosexuality.

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:54.660
Which American Psychiatric Association wrote
that it's no longer a disease,

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:56.420
it's only a disease if you don't like it.

00:02:56.420 --> 00:02:58.740
At the time that Szasz was writing,

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:01.940
lots of people were locked up who posed no
danger to other people

00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:04.900
and possibly not even dangerous to themselves
in any way.

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:09.520
And might be subjected to what today look
like horrifying treatments

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:12.480
but at the time were considered to be the
height of science.

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:18.000
Here the skull is thin enough to transmit
light

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:21.220
and it usually can be perforated easily by
the sharp instrument

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:23.240
without damage to any important structures.

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:27.100
But the man who discovered lobotomy and got
the Nobel prize

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:31.140
did on people who didn't want to be lobotomized,

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:33.930
tens of thousands of Americans were subjected
to lobotomy

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:35.579
did they want to be lobotomized?

00:03:35.579 --> 00:03:37.920
Lobotomy, if you take a sharp object

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:41.430
and stick it up through someone's eye socket
and jiggle it around in the brain,

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:46.580
this person who used to be troublesome and
disruptive will calm down often.

00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:50.800
Maybe what you end up with somebody who is
now less troublesome to other people

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:52.860
but is impaired for the rest of his life.

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:56.400
And you look back and you think how could
that have happened?

00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:00.060
Like how did they think that was a legitimate
medical treatment?

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:01.900
That was the context.

00:04:01.910 --> 00:04:05.230
Szasz helped to inspire the deinstitutionalization
movement

00:04:05.230 --> 00:04:09.180
that started in the 1950s and hit it's stride
in the 60s.

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:12.060
The time has come for a great national effort,

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:17.880
nearly half of the 530,000 persons in our
state mental hospitals

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:24.240
are in institutions with over 3000 patients,
getting little or no individual treatment.

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:29.000
Many of these institutions have less than
half of the professional staff required.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:34.080
45% of them have been hospitalized for ten
years or more.

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:40.080
If we launch a broad new health program now,
it will be possible within a decade

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:45.500
to reduce the populations now under custodial
care by up to 50%.

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:49.320
Mental hospitals started emptying out and
closing down.

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:53.440
But critics say deinstitutionalization was
a disaster,

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:57.480
dumping thousands of mental patients onto
the streets with no plan.

00:04:57.480 --> 00:05:02.060
Even Szasz criticized the way deinstitutionalization
unfolded.

00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:04.720
He thought it sort of doubled the injustice.

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:08.500
That those people were not offered the choice
to stay,

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:10.520
this is their home they're being thrown out
of now.

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:12.080
So he thought that was wrong.

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:16.730
The number of psychiatric hospital beds available
dropped by 95%

00:05:16.730 --> 00:05:21.700
between 1955 and 2005 according to the treatment
advocacy center.

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:26.700
Starting in 1960 as the mental health population
in hospitals declined,

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:30.440
the number of mentally ill people in jails
and prisons shot up.

00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:35.740
A 2011 Supreme Court case filed against the
California Bureau of Prisons

00:05:35.740 --> 00:05:38.090
revealed that some mentally ill inmates

00:05:38.090 --> 00:05:42.100
were held in telephone booth sized solitary
cells.

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:46.240
Some reformers say it's time to cast aside
Thomas Szasz

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:49.040
and his attack on the mental health establishment.

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:52.180
I got to say Szasz has been totally disproven

00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:58.100
it's kind of like believing the church's teachings
on an Earth centric universe.

00:05:58.100 --> 00:06:02.710
DJJaffe first became aware of the enormous
problems in America's mental health system

00:06:02.710 --> 00:06:06.440
after his sister in law was diagnosed with
schizophrenia

00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:10.400
and it proved enormously challenging to get
her the treatment she needed.

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:16.070
My sister in law thought that people across
the street were talking about her,

00:06:16.070 --> 00:06:19.410
the FBI planted a transmitter in her head.

00:06:19.410 --> 00:06:22.120
There are a section of the mental health industry

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:28.500
that believes being psychotic and delusional
is a right to be protected

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:31.020
rather than an illness to be treated.

00:06:31.020 --> 00:06:36.520
Being psychotic and delusional is not an exercise
of free will,

00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:39.940
it's the inability to exercise free will.

00:06:39.940 --> 00:06:44.340
And medications can free people from the Bastille
of their psychosis

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:46.920
and enable them to engage in society.

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:51.180
Jaffe says that the Szasz did offer a valid
critique of overreach

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:54.440
and over-diagnosis in the mental health industry.

00:06:54.440 --> 00:07:00.120
There are pieces of these anti-psychiatrists
which are true.

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:05.820
When you talk about someone with mild depression,
mild ADHD

00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:09.940
is it so abnormal as to be classified as a
disorder?

00:07:09.940 --> 00:07:15.440
And we are certainly over-diagnosing and medicalizing
that group.

00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:18.940
People have gotten the sense that he had good
critique at the time but it's sort of outmoded

00:07:18.940 --> 00:07:20.640
it really isn't outmoded, I mean

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:25.080
if anything the territory claimed by psychiatry
is expanding more and more.

00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:28.500
Look at all the other things that are covered
by the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:30.200
which is the psychiatric bible, right?

00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:33.300
But when it comes to the 4% of the population

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:39.160
that the National Institute of Mental Health
characterizes as having a serious mental illness

00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:42.990
Jaffe says healthcare professionals need far
more power

00:07:42.990 --> 00:07:47.480
to involuntarily commit patients to dedicated
treatment centers.

00:07:47.480 --> 00:07:50.040
The civil commitment law is not good enough,

00:07:50.040 --> 00:07:54.820
it basically requires someone to become danger
to self or others,

00:07:54.820 --> 00:07:58.920
we should prevent people from becoming danger
to self or others

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:02.660
not require them to become danger to self
or others.

00:08:02.660 --> 00:08:07.360
Jaffe also advocates for a policy called,
assisted outpatient treatment.

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:11.140
New York passed Kendra's law in November 1999

00:08:11.140 --> 00:08:14.020
after Goldstein's murder of Kendra Webdale.

00:08:14.020 --> 00:08:18.840
It allows a judge to order outpatient treatment
for anyone with a pychiatric diagnosis

00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:23.760
who's recently been jailed or hospitalized
as a result of going untreated

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:28.640
and who the court determines will likely deteriorate
further without intervention.

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:33.260
And I would suggest that it is a libertarian's
dream.

00:08:33.260 --> 00:08:38.840
It has lowered rates of homelessness, arrest,
incarceration, needless hospitalization

00:08:38.840 --> 00:08:44.219
in the 70% range for the most seriously mentally
ill.

00:08:44.219 --> 00:08:49.910
And from a libertarian perspective they maintain
more of their freedoms than the alternatives

00:08:49.910 --> 00:08:53.620
which are inpatient commitment or incarceration.

00:08:53.620 --> 00:08:58.220
This idea that we should just abandon the
seriously mentally ill,

00:08:58.220 --> 00:09:00.900
who don't recognize their need for treatment,

00:09:00.900 --> 00:09:06.820
serves neither the liberty interest of the
individual, the financial interest of society,

00:09:06.820 --> 00:09:10.580
or the interest of trying to keep the public
safe.

00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:15.340
When you're trying to get help for someone,

00:09:15.340 --> 00:09:19.120
especially for Misty who didn't feel like
she needed help,

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:20.860
it's very very difficult.

00:09:20.860 --> 00:09:23.360
Linda Mayo who lives in Modesto California

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:27.640
has been advocating for the enactment of Laura's
law in her home county.

00:09:27.640 --> 00:09:31.040
She's the mother of twin daughters, both with
severe mental illnesses

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:34.900
and it's through their experiences that she
became an advocate

00:09:34.900 --> 00:09:39.440
for strengthening the involuntary commitment
and compulsory treatment laws.

00:09:39.440 --> 00:09:43.139
It's not a panacea, it's not going to help
everyone,

00:09:43.139 --> 00:09:47.019
but the people who refuse treatment and consistently
refuse treatment

00:09:47.020 --> 00:09:52.860
and who are in and out of the hospital, in
and out of jail, homeless, suicidal

00:09:52.860 --> 00:09:54.200
it can help them.

00:09:54.200 --> 00:09:57.760
Mayo believes that the legal obstacles to
involuntary commitment

00:09:57.760 --> 00:09:59.820
could've cost her daughter her life.

00:09:59.820 --> 00:10:05.800
One day five years ago Misty, who was 38 and
has schizoaffective disorder vanished.

00:10:05.800 --> 00:10:09.200
Mayo discovered that after collecting her
$200 paycheck

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:11.900
she had boarded a bus to Los Angeles.

00:10:11.900 --> 00:10:16.740
I was frantic, she was in a big city that
she was unfamiliar with

00:10:16.740 --> 00:10:19.579
and we had no family or friends there, very
little money,

00:10:19.579 --> 00:10:22.209
I thought '$200 that's going to go fast.'

00:10:22.209 --> 00:10:27.389
She had already been sleeping in the parks
here before she left

00:10:27.389 --> 00:10:29.240
and roaming the streets

00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:34.880
so I thought, in LA, it scared me more.

00:10:34.880 --> 00:10:40.660
Another week and a half went by, finally the
phone rang, it was LAPD.

00:10:40.660 --> 00:10:46.460
She was in a neighborhood and sitting on a
cliff and the neighbors were concerned about her.

00:10:46.460 --> 00:10:52.340
In Mayo's view, Misty posed a threat not
only to herself but to others as well.

00:10:52.340 --> 00:10:55.760
Mayo later found out that during the time
she was missing

00:10:55.760 --> 00:10:58.940
Misty had been arrested in a shopping mall.

00:10:58.940 --> 00:11:01.940
I can't tell you what was going through her
head

00:11:01.940 --> 00:11:05.800
but she was trying to take a baby out of a
baby carriage.

00:11:05.800 --> 00:11:10.800
And the mother said no and Misty didn't heed
that.

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:12.640
So she was arrested.

00:11:12.640 --> 00:11:16.640
For years Misty had been refusing therapy
and medication.

00:11:16.640 --> 00:11:21.340
After her arrest the state had legal grounds
for forcing her to comply with treatment.

00:11:21.340 --> 00:11:24.380
Linda believes this saved Misty's life.

00:11:24.380 --> 00:11:30.720
I was very happy that she was being allowed
to be in the hospital for a longer period of time

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:32.840
that would require some stabilization.

00:11:32.840 --> 00:11:35.920
Laura's law in her view creates a legal path

00:11:35.930 --> 00:11:40.569
to getting seriously mentally ill patients
like Misty the help they need

00:11:40.569 --> 00:11:43.820
before they pose a threat to themselves or
others.

00:11:43.820 --> 00:11:50.720
This is one step saying 'ok we want to catch
you in a court order before it's a crime,

00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:54.200
before it's hospitalization, before it's homelessness.'

00:11:54.200 --> 00:11:58.120
But some mental health advocates say that
Laura's law and similar bills

00:11:58.120 --> 00:12:02.200
violate the rights of even seriously mentally
ill patients.

00:12:02.200 --> 00:12:07.140
There's a big perception in my community that
once they get their claws on you,

00:12:07.140 --> 00:12:11.260
just like law enforcement, they're not letting
you go if they can help it.

00:12:11.260 --> 00:12:14.860
Richard Krzyzanowski who himself has suffered
from severe depression

00:12:14.860 --> 00:12:18.740
is a patients rights advocate who has worked
for LA county

00:12:18.740 --> 00:12:22.860
representing mentally ill individuals committed
against their will.

00:12:22.860 --> 00:12:27.600
Some of the most dis-empowering and dangerous
words anyone can say is

00:12:27.600 --> 00:12:30.740
'we're doing this for your own good.'

00:12:30.740 --> 00:12:32.339
you know, 'we're here to help you.'

00:12:32.339 --> 00:12:34.639
But if I'm not asking for help.

00:12:34.639 --> 00:12:39.339
When you see somebody walking down the street,
screaming 'I am the messiah.'

00:12:39.339 --> 00:12:44.769
It's not because they think they are the messiah,
they know they're the messiah.

00:12:44.769 --> 00:12:50.960
They are unaware that they are ill and we
should be delivering treatment to them.

00:12:50.960 --> 00:12:54.400
But Krzyzanowski says that mental health professionals
and reformers

00:12:54.410 --> 00:12:58.050
often abuse the concept of 'too sick to know
you are sick'

00:12:58.050 --> 00:13:00.440
to violate the rights of the mentally ill.

00:13:00.440 --> 00:13:03.440
It's a way of saying 'I don't have to listen
to you.'

00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:06.840
or more to the point 'you're now voiceless.'

00:13:06.840 --> 00:13:11.680
The whole involuntary treatment is a hard
balance.

00:13:11.680 --> 00:13:16.220
No one wants involuntary, all the advocates
that I have talked to,

00:13:16.220 --> 00:13:20.320
this is the last thing that we want.

00:13:20.320 --> 00:13:24.820
But there are cases that require it.

00:13:24.820 --> 00:13:28.500
And Krzyzanowski says that even though the
state institutions are gone

00:13:28.509 --> 00:13:32.050
to this day, many psychiatric patients are
locked in facilities

00:13:32.050 --> 00:13:34.529
for weeks or even months at a time

00:13:34.529 --> 00:13:39.780
under court orders giving legal power over
financial and healthcare decisions to a third party

00:13:39.780 --> 00:13:42.319
under an arrangement called conservatorship.

00:13:42.320 --> 00:13:48.480
The most difficult calls we would get, especially
during anybody's holiday season.

00:13:48.480 --> 00:13:53.619
Would be people on conservatorship, who are
locked away somewhere

00:13:53.620 --> 00:13:56.440
they were able to get a phone call

00:13:56.440 --> 00:13:59.880
and you just felt like you were talking to
one person alone

00:13:59.880 --> 00:14:02.100
like they've been locked away in a dungeon

00:14:02.100 --> 00:14:05.220
and they hadn't seen, like, the light for
months.

00:14:05.220 --> 00:14:09.220
Another problem with the idea of bringing
back state mental hospitals

00:14:09.220 --> 00:14:14.199
is that in the current system commitment often
exacerbates symptoms.

00:14:14.199 --> 00:14:17.480
A study published in the medical journal Psychiatric
Services

00:14:17.480 --> 00:14:22.290
found that alarmingly high numbers of patients
admitted to one state run mental health system

00:14:22.290 --> 00:14:27.760
witnessed traumatic events, were secluded
or restrained and even suffered physical assault.

00:14:27.760 --> 00:14:30.839
We can change the whole historic dynamic

00:14:30.840 --> 00:14:34.520
of the use of coercion in people with serious
mental illnesses.

00:14:34.520 --> 00:14:38.280
Psychiatrist Scott Zeller is the founder of the Alameda
Model,

00:14:38.280 --> 00:14:42.380
a movement dedicated to reforming how mental
health institutions operate.

00:14:42.380 --> 00:14:47.060
It focuses on getting psychiatric patients
out of hospital emergency rooms

00:14:47.060 --> 00:14:48.380
as quickly  as possible

00:14:48.380 --> 00:14:54.149
and into dedicated mental facilities that
respect their right to personal space and freedom

00:14:54.149 --> 00:14:56.860
even if they've been involuntarily committed.

00:14:56.860 --> 00:14:59.140
Typically if you're in a psychiatric emergency,

00:14:59.140 --> 00:15:04.400
you're very paranoid, or you're very frightened
or you're very despondent, or you're very 
 anxious.

00:15:04.400 --> 00:15:09.240
And imagine going into the general ER with
those kind of symptoms

00:15:09.240 --> 00:15:12.079
and basically being put into a small space

00:15:12.079 --> 00:15:14.529
and says 'you're going to have to stay here
and you can't move.'

00:15:14.529 --> 00:15:16.480
You know, people tend to get worse in ERs.

00:15:16.480 --> 00:15:19.399
The Providence Medical Center in Saint Peter
California

00:15:19.400 --> 00:15:24.220
which uses Zeller's approach is a stand alone
building just down the road from a hospital.

00:15:24.220 --> 00:15:28.420
Unlike in hospitals which are often lacking
in on call psychiatrists

00:15:28.430 --> 00:15:32.829
staff are able to medicate and stabilize patients
around the clock

00:15:32.829 --> 00:15:36.929
sometimes through the use of telepsychiatry
if a doctor isn't on site

00:15:36.929 --> 00:15:41.780
with the goal of discharging them for outpatient
treatment within the first 24 hours of admission.

00:15:41.780 --> 00:15:45.640
People can move about, nobody's telling them
'get back in that corner' or,

00:15:45.640 --> 00:15:50.860
what we find is the vast majority of the time,
these folks are going to get better

00:15:50.860 --> 00:15:54.480
within ten, fifteen, twenty hours tops.

00:15:54.480 --> 00:15:58.460
And the idea that we were holding them for
three days to send them to a hospital

00:15:58.460 --> 00:16:03.160
makes so little sense when you could have
had them home the same day.

00:16:03.160 --> 00:16:07.200
The average psychiatric emergency patient
in a general medical ER

00:16:07.200 --> 00:16:12.540
it may be 25-50% of them being physically
restrained,

00:16:12.540 --> 00:16:14.860
leather restraints around wrists, ankles.

00:16:14.860 --> 00:16:19.340
In an empath unit we're talking about far
less than 1%.

00:16:19.340 --> 00:16:25.540
People respond very well to a supportive,
interacting, nurturing environment

00:16:25.540 --> 00:16:29.320
not so much to a coercive cold environment,
go figure.

00:16:29.320 --> 00:16:33.040
Is social division within the province of
medicine?

00:16:33.040 --> 00:16:35.379
Zeller says there's one lesson from Szasz

00:16:35.380 --> 00:16:39.640
that family members of the seriously mentally
ill should internalize:

00:16:39.640 --> 00:16:45.100
there's no level of coercion that can force
some people to conform with societal norms.

00:16:45.100 --> 00:16:48.740
As long as they don't pose a danger to themselves
or others

00:16:48.740 --> 00:16:52.700
in some cases the best path forward is acceptance.

00:16:52.700 --> 00:16:55.920
If someone says 'I want to live in a tent
downtown.'

00:16:55.920 --> 00:16:58.260
Pretty much you have to let them.

00:16:58.260 --> 00:17:02.140
It's really really difficult to determine
when you should believe

00:17:02.140 --> 00:17:05.580
that they no longer have the right to decide
for themselves.

00:17:05.580 --> 00:17:10.600
I think that we should be overly safe on the
side of protecting individual rights.

00:17:10.600 --> 00:17:13.300
I can't have pictures up in my house

00:17:13.300 --> 00:17:17.290
of when they were babies in junior high and
high school and college

00:17:17.290 --> 00:17:20.820
it's the shine, the light in their eyes that
I miss.

00:17:20.820 --> 00:17:29.420
That sense of joy and fun and happiness is
out of the eyes

00:17:29.420 --> 00:17:31.720
and that makes me really sad.

